hazync#205 — recover a pubkey's Y from a witness HINT, verified, instead of a modular square root.

⛔ EXPERIMENTAL, OPT-IN, AND IT MOVES METHOD_ID. Applied only when HAZYNC_LIFTX_HINT=1.

secp256k1_ge_set_xo_var already computes x3 = x^3 + 7 (two field ops) and already normalises and
fixes the parity of y afterwards. The ONLY expensive step is secp256k1_fe_sqrt, measured natively at
264x a field multiply and at 1.416 G cycles on block 962,000 — 9.83% of the block and ~45% of all
post-#139 work, which patches/0005 does not touch because it lives outside secp256k1_ecmult.

⛔ 2026-08-31: those figures predate patches/0012's coprocessor field backend, which makes every
multiply and square in the sqrt chain ~14x cheaper. Re-measured on the same block WITH the backend,
fe_sqrt is 241,023,621 cycles CUMULATIVE = 6.17%. This lever is worth at most that when the two are
combined, not 9.83%.

This replaces that sqrt with: take y from the hint, check y^2 == x3. Those two conditions plus the
EXISTING parity fixup below determine y uniquely, so the check accepts exactly what the sqrt would
have produced. An x not on the curve admits no y satisfying y^2 == x3, so it falls through to the
real sqrt and fails there as before. secp256k1 has prime order and no point of order 2, so y == 0
cannot arise.

⇒ This is ADVICE-AND-VERIFY, not a reimplementation of consensus arithmetic: no group arithmetic is
substituted and libsecp's own code decides the result. The fidelity posture is therefore strictly
better than #139's — there is no equivalence surface to argue about, only a plumbing obligation.

A missing or wrong hint is never load-bearing: ret stays 0 and the original sqrt runs.

